{
  "dom_id": "step-638292",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line",
  "problem": "The region below enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt x$ and $y=x$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $y=1.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/362a92640ed68dfd5fa85f0338d95233003e9f8a1b059a5dffa2cda243a1411b.png)",
  "worked_solution": "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n \t\tx & =\\sqrt x  \\[3pt]\n \t\tx-\\sqrt x & = 0 \\[3pt]\n \t\t\\sqrt x \\left(\\sqrt x - 1\\right) &= 0 \\[3pt]\n \t\t\tx& = 0, 1\n\t}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/5456bed18c03e823c8e9388571b5671f5f476f5caa1085793314b57080b2b07c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ and\n\n- *leaves* though the outer curver $y=x.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/5136903876ed5ea811ebf28e0da905b5029b2c9311527fce9d0fed8e7bd1dd46.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(1)-(x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1-x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/c850643bb1c315d0516eff55d6270078504c1c98687cfac40e0c9b4b2c771b7c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(1)-(\\sqrt{x})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1-\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nV &= {\\color{blue}V_{\\text{outer}}} - {\\color{brown}V_{\\text{inner}}} \\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\pi \\int_0^1 [1-x]^2 - \\pi \\int_0^1 [1-\\sqrt x]^2\\,\\textrm d x \\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\pi \\int_0^1 \\left((1-x)^2 - (1-\\sqrt x)^2\\right) \\,\\textrm d x \\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\pi \\int_0^1 (x^2-3x+2\\sqrt x) \\,\\,\\textrm d x \\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\pi\\left. \\left( \\dfrac {1} 3 x^3 - \\dfrac{3}{2} x^2 + \\dfrac{4}{3} x^{3/2 } \\ \\right)\\right|_0^1\\[5pt] \n \t\t& =  \\pi \\left[\\left( \\dfrac {1}3 (1)^3 - \\dfrac{3}{2}(1)^2  + \\dfrac{4 }{3}(1)^{3/2 } \\right) - (0) \\right]\\[5pt]\n \t\t& =  \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{1}3 - \\dfrac{3}{2} +\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\dfrac {\\pi}{6} .\n \t\t}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
